// @run
// @log 1
// @log a
// THE UNION LEAF, NOW RUNNING (D702). The file keeps its `error-` name so the headers that cite
// it still resolve; what it pins is no longer an error.
//
// ITS HISTORY IN ONE LINE. An un-annotated NESTED literal whose INNER element is a value union
// (`[[1, "a"]]`) was `vl check`-clean INVALID WASM two changes ago, then a loud
// `nested arrays are not supported`, and is now the answer the ANNOTATED spelling has always
// given: `const nested: (i32 | string)[][] = [[1, "a"]]` prints 1 then a.
//
// THE MECHANISM IT DESCRIBED IS THE ONE THAT MOVED. `arrLitIsRef`'s nested rung claims an outer
// literal only when the INNER one is NOT itself a ref list:
//
//     if e0 is ArrayLit { if !arrLitIsRef(a.arrElems[0]) { return true } }
//
// Registering the inner literal's union makes the inner a ref list (kind 2), so the outer
// stopped being claimed by THAT rung and fell to the i32 list, where the inner list's ref hit
// that path's floor. The rung is unchanged; what changed is where the fall-through lands. The
// arena rung at the bottom of `arrLitIsRef` now claims the literal (its element renders and the
// ref-list layer can name it), and the matching kind-9 rungs in `arrLitElemKind` /
// `arrLitElemName` key it from the ARENA rather than from the leaf probes — which are one level
// short for a ref inner and would have keyed `i32[]` over a `(ref $rlWrap)` backing.
//
// Its own header said supporting this "needs a ref-list-of-ref-lists element kind and an inner
// row interned at a strictly lower slot". Both are now what happens: kind 9, interned
// inner-first through `ensureRefElemTy`.
//
// TOP-LEVEL ON PURPOSE, kept from the original: wrapped in a function the same program used to
// report `unsupported for-in iterable` instead, because the outer `for`-in over a list the
// emitter never typed failed first.
const nested = [[1, "a"]]
for inner in nested {
  for v in inner {
    if v is i32 { print(v) } else { print(v) }
  }
}
